  </div>

  <h3>1(c) High-Availability Failover &amp; Resilient Nginx Configuration (1.5 Marks)</h3>
  <p>Continuous 15-VU traffic was sent for 25 seconds while container <code>mess_backend_2</code> was stopped mid-flight at second 4:</p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Configuration State</th>
      <th>Nginx Failover Setting</th>
      <th>Failed Requests (502 Gateway)</th>
      <th>Success Rate</th>
      <th>User Impact</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Before Configuration</strong></td>
      <td><code>proxy_next_upstream off;</code></td>
      <td><span class="badge-danger">3 Failed Requests</span></td>
      <td>99.87%</td>
      <td>Students received HTTP 502 Bad Gateway errors</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>After Configuration</strong></td>
      <td><code>proxy_next_upstream error timeout http_502...</code></td>
      <td><span class="badge-success">0 Failed Requests</span></td>
      <td><strong>100.00%</strong></td>
      <td><strong>Transparent instant retry; 0 user downtime</strong></td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <p><strong>Nginx Hardened Configuration:</strong></p>
  <pre>proxy_next_upstream error timeout invalid_header http_500 http_502 http_503 http_504;
proxy_next_upstream_tries 3;
proxy_next_upstream_timeout 5s;
proxy_connect_timeout 1s;</pre>

  <div class="screenshot-box">
    <strong>📸 ATTACH SCREENSHOT 5 HERE</strong>
    <span>Figure 1.5: k6 terminal output for Before Failover test showing 3 failed requests (502 Bad Gateway).</span>
  </div>

  <div class="screenshot-box">
    <strong>📸 ATTACH SCREENSHOT 6 HERE</strong>
    <span>Figure 1.6: k6 terminal output for After Failover test showing 0 failed requests (100% success rate).</span>
  </div>

  <h3>1(d) System Architecture Diagram &amp; Edge/Fog Analysis (1 Mark)</h3>

  <div class="screenshot-box">
    <strong>🖼️ INSERT ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM HERE (images/architecture_diagram.jpg)</strong>
    <span>Figure 1.7: Cloud Architecture Diagram with Vercel Frontend, Nginx (Port 80), 3 Backends (Ports 5001, 5002, 5003), MongoDB Atlas, Render, and Edge/Fog Dining Node (in Bright Orange).</span>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Actual Component Names &amp; Ports:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Frontend:</strong> MessHub Web Client hosted on <strong>Vercel</strong> (<code>mess-feedback-backend.vercel.app</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Load Balancer:</strong> Nginx container <strong><code>mess_nginx_lb</code></strong> on Port <strong>80:80</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Backend Containers:</strong> <strong><code>mess_backend_1</code></strong> (Port 5001:5000), <strong><code>mess_backend_2</code></strong> (Port 5002:5000), <strong><code>mess_backend_3</code></strong> (Port 5003:5000).</li>
    <li><strong>Cloud Database:</strong> <strong>MongoDB Atlas</strong> (<code>Cluster0.tukfqik.mongodb.net</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Assignment 1 Cloud Hosting:</strong> <strong>Render Web Service</strong> (<code>mess-feedback-backend-1z0s.onrender.com</code>).</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Edge/Fog Computing Component (Highlighted in Orange):</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why Chosen for Mess Domain:</strong> Dining halls suffer from severe cellular dead zones and Wi-Fi throttling due to reinforced concrete basements and 1,000+ simultaneous students.</li>
    <li><strong>Sub-Millisecond Local Lookups:</strong> An on-premise <strong>Fog Dining Gateway</strong> located at the mess entrance turnstiles caches the daily menu locally in Redis/SQLite, returning queries in <strong>&lt;5 ms</strong> instead of traversing 200 ms external cloud WANs.</li>
    <li><strong>Offline Queueing &amp; Cloud Bursting:</strong> Feedback submitted during internet dropouts is queued locally in the Fog Node and synchronized asynchronously to MongoDB Atlas once WAN connectivity recovers. When local CPU load exceeds 80%, cloud bursting overflows surge traffic to serverless cloud runtimes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Question 2 — Secure Your Application (5 Marks)</h2>

  <h3>2(a) Sensitive Data &amp; Salted Bcrypt Password Hashing (1 Mark)</h3>
  <p><strong>Sensitive Data Identified:</strong> Student Roll Numbers, Full Names, Mobile Phone Numbers, Administrator/Caterer Passwords, and Student Hygiene/Dietary Feedback Records.</p>
  <p><strong>Cryptographic Protection:</strong> All plain-text credentials were replaced with <strong>10-round salted bcrypt hashes</strong> (<code>bcrypt.genSalt(10)</code>) stored directly in MongoDB Atlas.</p>

